\begin_layout Standard
Three conclusions follow from these results.
 The first is that 
\emph on
the real processor reproduces the qualitative behavior of every experiment
\emph default
.
 The correlated outcomes of the Bell state,
 the reduced length of the Bloch vectors in entangled pairs,
 the growth of the witness along Experiment 4 and the sign of the dominant correlator in Experiment 5 are all visible on the hardware,
 with small deviations from the simulation.
 The second is that 
\emph on
the noise is moderate
\emph default
.
 The noise index stays between 1.7% and 6.5%,
 and the lowest value belongs to the experiment without an entangling gate,
 which agrees with the fact that two-qubit gates are the main source of error in present devices.
 The third conclusion concerns a 
\emph on
limit of the instrument
\emph default
.
 In Experiment 5 the witness remains at the separable bound of 1,
 even though the state is maximally entangled,
 so a single indicator cannot replace the full set of correlators and vectors.
 The entanglement of that state is recognized only by combining the strong ⟨YY⟩ correlation with Bloch vectors close to zero length.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
These results must be read with caution.

\emph default
 Each figure comes from a single run on a single device,
 so the values give the order of magnitude of the noise and not a statistical measurement.
 
\emph on
A different run,
 or the same device on another day,
 could give different numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Practical limits of access to quantum hardware
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:6.3 Practical limits of access to quantum hardware"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Working on a real quantum processor requires planning that a simulator does not.
 A new IBM account gives 10 minutes of free quantum computing time,
 and all my tests needed about 15 minutes.
 The jobs also wait in a queue,
 whose duration is not under the control of the user,
 and the free account is limited in the resources it can use,
 as described in Chapter 4
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vpageref
reference "sec:4 Experiments on a real quantum computer"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "range"

\end_inset

.
 In the game the quantum time is consumed quickly,
 because a deck is generated by repeating a four-qubit circuit with one measurement per run and discarding the cards already obtained.
 About 54 runs are needed on average to complete the sixteen cards,
 and each run is a separate job.
 Each matched pair then requires further jobs for the experiment circuit and its observables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a person who wants to explore the quantum world,
 the practical consequence is a way of working.
 
\emph on
Development and debugging should be done on the simulator
\emph default
,
 which is instant and exact,
 and the hardware should be kept for a small number of final runs whose results are worth the time.
 The design adopted here follows this idea,
 since a single line of the configuration file switches between the two backends,
 and the same code runs unchanged.
 The limitation also suggests a direction for improvement,
 namely a deck generation that uses fewer jobs.
\end_layout

