\begin_layout Standard
The common point of these cases is that the error was not a failure to produce an answer,
 
\emph on
but a plausible answer that did not match the physics,
 the code or the data.

\emph default
 The method that worked was to treat the assistants as fast collaborators whose statements are verified:
 asking for the reasoning,
 reading the code,
 comparing with the ideal values,
 using a second tool as a reviewer,
 and asking again when a result looked wrong.
 This verification required enough understanding of the subject to notice a problem,
 which is a reason why the learning process and the use of AI cannot be separated.
 
\emph on
The conclusion for modern projects is that AI is an effective tool when it is monitored,
 and that the responsibility for the correctness of the work remains with the author.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What this work has meant for learning
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:6.5 What this work has meant for learning"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the beginning of this project I did not know quantum computing at all.
 This thesis follows the path that took me from that starting point to a game that generates its cards from quantum measurements,
 to five experiments on entanglement and to their execution on a real processor.
 Several choices made the path shorter.
 Building a game gave me a concrete object to reason about,
 instead of an abstract formalism.
 The visual outputs,
 Bloch spheres,
 density matrices and correlator charts,
 allowed me to see what each state looks like before I could describe it with mathematics.
 The simulator and the hardware used identical code,
 so the difference between the ideal and the real behavior became an observation and not just a statement in a textbook.
 The IBM course and the Qiskit documentation gave the theoretical base,
 and the AI assistants acted as tutors,
 provided that their explanations were checked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting material is also reusable.
 The code,
 the circuits and the descriptions are archived in a public repository with a DOI,
 so a reader with the same starting point can follow the same steps,
 repeat the experiments and change them.
 I consider this the main contribution of the work:
 a method and a set of tools that lower the entry barrier to a subject that appears difficult at first,
 and not only a collection of results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Limitations and future work
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:6.6 Limitations and future work"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The work has several limits!
 The hardware results come from single runs on one device,
 and each experiment uses only two qubits.
 The entanglement witness is not sufficient for every state,
 as Experiment 5 showed.
 The generation of the deck is expensive on real hardware.
 The educational effect of the game has been described,
 but it has not been measured with learners.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Several developments are possible,
 the deck generation could be redesigned to use fewer jobs.
 State tomography could be added on hardware,
 to reconstruct the density matrix at the cost of more quantum time.
 Error mitigation techniques could be applied to reduce the effect of noise,
 and the experiments could be repeated on different devices and on different days to obtain statistics.
 Larger entangled states with three or more qubits would extend the game to richer phenomena.
 A study with students would test whether the approach really shortens the learning path,
 which is the claim that this thesis can only support from a single experience.
\end_layout

